from extras import ex

ex('evidence-evolution',"Fossils form when hard parts are replaced by minerals in rock over millions of years. Older rock layers usually contain simpler fossils. The fossil record is incomplete because most dead organisms decay before they can fossilise.",
 [('What is a fossil?','The remains or traces of an organism preserved in rock.','A living animal.','A type of mineral.','Bones and shells can be preserved.'),
  ('Why is the fossil record incomplete?','Most organisms decay before they can fossilise.','Fossils are too large.','All fossils have been found.','Fossilisation needs special conditions.'),
  ('Similar DNA in two species suggests…','they share a common ancestor.','they are the same age.','they live in the same place.','DNA similarity shows relatedness.'),
  ('Lower rock layers are usually…','older.','younger.','the same age as the top.','Layers build up over time.')],
 [('What is evolution?','A change in the inherited features of a species over many generations.'),
  ('Give two types of evidence for evolution.','Fossils.~DNA similarities / antibiotic resistance in bacteria / comparing body structures.'),
  ('Explain why scientists cannot be completely sure how some species evolved.','The fossil record is incomplete.~Soft-bodied organisms rarely fossilise.~Many fossils have been destroyed by geological processes or not yet found.')])

ex('biodiversity',"High biodiversity makes an ecosystem more stable, because species depend on each other. Seed banks, zoos, nature reserves and breeding programmes help protect species. Reducing pollution and habitat loss also protects biodiversity.",
 [('What does biodiversity mean?','The variety of living things in an area.','The number of plants only.','The size of an animal.','It includes all species.'),
  ('What does a seed bank do?','Stores seeds so plant species are not lost.','Sells seeds for profit only.','Kills seeds.','Seeds can be planted in the future.'),
  ('Which is a main cause of species becoming endangered?','Habitat loss.','Too much rain.','Seed banks.','Destroying habitats removes food and shelter.'),
  ('Why is high biodiversity good for an ecosystem?','It is more stable if one species declines.','It makes animals bigger.','It removes all predators.','Food webs have more alternatives.')],
 [('What does extinct mean?','No members of the species are left alive.'),
  ('Give two ways humans can protect endangered species.','Nature reserves / protecting habitats.~Breeding programmes in zoos / seed banks / laws against hunting.'),
  ('Explain why losing one species can affect many others in a food web.','Species depend on each other for food.~Predators lose a food source and may decline.~Its prey may increase and over-eat plants, changing the whole ecosystem.')])

ex('inheritance',"Human body cells have 46 chromosomes in 23 pairs; one of each pair comes from each parent. Sex cells (sperm and egg) have only 23 chromosomes. The double-helix structure of DNA was worked out by Watson, Crick, Franklin and Wilkins.",
 [('How many chromosomes are in a normal human body cell?','46.','23.','92.','23 pairs.'),
  ('How many chromosomes are in a human sperm cell?','23.','46.','12.','Sex cells have half.'),
  ('What is a gene?','A section of DNA that codes for a characteristic.','A whole cell.','A type of protein.','Genes are on chromosomes.'),
  ('Whose X-ray images helped show DNA is a double helix?','Rosalind Franklin.','Charles Darwin.','Isaac Newton.','Photo 51 was crucial.')],
 [('Where in the cell are chromosomes found?','In the nucleus.'),
  ('Explain why a sperm cell has 23 chromosomes, not 46.','At fertilisation the sperm joins with an egg that also has 23.~So the fertilised egg has 46, the normal number.'),
  ('Explain why children look a bit like both parents but not exactly like either.','Children get half their chromosomes from each parent.~So they inherit a mixture of genes from both.~Different combinations of alleles give different features.')])

ex('dna-practical',"Mash the fruit with washing-up liquid and salt to break open cells. Filter the mixture, then gently pour cold ethanol on top. White strands of DNA appear where the layers meet.",
 [('What does washing-up liquid do in DNA extraction?','Breaks down cell membranes.','Makes DNA visible as blue.','Kills the fruit.','Detergent breaks apart membranes.'),
  ('Why is ice-cold ethanol used?','DNA does not dissolve in it, so it clumps and can be seen.','It heats the mixture.','It colours the DNA.','DNA comes out of solution.'),
  ('Strawberries are good for this practical because…','they have many copies of their DNA in each cell.','they have no DNA.','they are red.','Lots of DNA makes it easier to see.'),
  ('What do the white strands contain?','Many DNA molecules clumped together.','A single gene.','Protein only.','One DNA molecule is too small to see.')],
 [('Where is DNA found in a cell?','In the nucleus (in chromosomes).'),
  ('Give the purpose of two steps in extracting DNA.','Mashing with detergent breaks open the cells and nuclei.~Adding cold ethanol makes the DNA come out of solution so it can be seen.'),
  ('Explain why you cannot see the double helix in the extracted strands.','Each DNA molecule is far too thin to see.~The strands are millions of molecules tangled together.~You would need very powerful methods to see the shape of one molecule.')])

ex('cross',"A dominant allele is shown with a capital letter, such as B; a recessive allele with a lower-case letter, such as b. A recessive feature only appears if both alleles are recessive (bb). A Punnett square shows the possible offspring.",
 [('If B (brown eyes) is dominant, what eye colour does Bb give?','Brown.','Blue.','Green.','One dominant allele is enough.'),
  ('Which genotype shows the recessive feature?','bb.','Bb.','BB.','Both alleles must be recessive.'),
  ('Two Bb parents have a child. What is the chance it is bb?','25%.','50%.','75%.','1 out of 4 boxes.'),
  ('What is a phenotype?','The feature that shows, such as eye colour.','The pair of alleles.','A chromosome.','Phenotype is what you can see or measure.')],
 [('What is an allele?','A different version of a gene.'),
  ('A homozygous dominant parent (BB) and a recessive parent (bb) have children. Give the possible genotype(s) and the phenotype.','All the children are Bb.~They all show the dominant feature (e.g. brown eyes).'),
  ('Two parents with brown eyes have a child with blue eyes. Explain how this is possible (B = brown, b = blue).','Both parents must be heterozygous, Bb.~Each parent can pass on a b allele.~The child inherits bb, so shows the recessive blue eyes (1 in 4 chance).')])

ex('gm',"Selective breeding has produced crops with bigger yields and cows that make more milk. Genetic modification can make crops resistant to pests or add vitamins, like golden rice. Some people worry about effects on wildlife and human health.",
 [('Choosing the best cows to breed for more milk is…','selective breeding.','genetic modification.','natural selection.','Humans choose the parents.'),
  ('Golden rice has been modified to contain…','more vitamin A (as beta-carotene).','more protein.','less water.','It helps prevent vitamin A deficiency.'),
  ('A concern about selective breeding is…','reduced variation, making disease more damaging.','animals become extinct immediately.','it is too quick.','Inbreeding reduces genetic variety.'),
  ('Genetic modification involves…','putting a gene from one organism into another.','choosing parents over many years.','feeding plants more.','Genes are moved directly.')],
 [('What is selective breeding?','Humans choosing parents with useful features to breed over many generations.'),
  ('Give one benefit and one concern about GM crops.','Benefit: pest resistance / higher yield / added vitamins.~Concern: effects on wildlife or food chains / possible health effects / genes spreading to wild plants.'),
  ('Describe how a farmer could breed sheep with thicker wool.','Choose the sheep with the thickest wool as parents.~Breed them together and select the offspring with the thickest wool.~Repeat over many generations.')])

ex('waves',"Wave speed = frequency × wavelength. Wavelength is the distance from one peak to the next. Water waves and light are transverse; sound is longitudinal.",
 [('A wave has frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 2 m. What is its speed?','10 m/s.','2.5 m/s.','7 m/s.','v = f × λ.'),
  ('What is the wavelength?','The distance from one peak to the next peak.','The height of a wave.','The number of waves per second.','Measured in metres.'),
  ('Which is a longitudinal wave?','Sound.','Light.','A water ripple.','Vibrations are along the direction of travel.'),
  ('What does a wave transfer?','Energy.','Matter from place to place.','Particles only.','The material stays roughly where it is.')],
 [('What is the amplitude of a wave?','The maximum distance a point moves from its rest position (the height of a peak).'),
  ('A sound wave has a frequency of 170 Hz and a wavelength of 2 m. Calculate its speed.','v = f × λ = 170 × 2.~= 340 m/s.'),
  ('Explain the difference between a transverse and a longitudinal wave, with an example of each.','In a transverse wave the vibrations are at right angles to the direction of travel, e.g. light or water waves.~In a longitudinal wave the vibrations are along the direction of travel, e.g. sound.~Both transfer energy without transferring the material.')])

ex('ultrasound',"Ultrasound is used to scan unborn babies because it is safe and does not harm cells. Ships use sonar to measure sea depth from the time an echo takes to return. Bats and dolphins use ultrasound echoes to find food.",
 [('Ultrasound has a frequency…','above 20,000 Hz.','below 20 Hz.','of exactly 1000 Hz.','Too high for humans to hear.'),
  ('Why is ultrasound used for pregnancy scans instead of X-rays?','It does not damage cells.','It is cheaper only.','It shows colours.','X-rays can harm the fetus.'),
  ('A sonar pulse returns after 2 s. Sound in water travels 1500 m/s. How deep is the sea?','1500 m.','3000 m.','750 m.','Distance there and back = 3000 m, so depth = 1500 m.'),
  ('Which animal uses ultrasound to hunt?','Bat.','Cow.','Snail.','Bats use echolocation.')],
 [('What is ultrasound?','Sound with a frequency above about 20,000 Hz (too high for humans to hear).'),
  ('A ship’s sonar pulse returns after 0.8 s. Sound travels at 1500 m/s in water. Calculate the depth.','Total distance = 1500 × 0.8 = 1200 m.~Depth = 1200 ÷ 2 = 600 m.'),
  ('Explain how a bat uses ultrasound to find an insect.','The bat sends out ultrasound pulses.~The pulses reflect off the insect as echoes.~The time taken for the echo to return tells the bat how far away the insect is.')])

ex('em',"In order of increasing frequency: radio, microwave, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma. Higher-frequency waves carry more energy and can be more harmful. All electromagnetic waves travel at the speed of light in a vacuum.",
 [('Which EM wave is used to cook food in a microwave oven?','Microwaves.','X-rays.','Radio waves.','Water molecules absorb them.'),
  ('Which EM wave can cause sunburn?','Ultraviolet.','Radio.','Infrared only.','UV can damage skin cells.'),
  ('Which EM wave has the highest frequency?','Gamma rays.','Radio waves.','Visible light.','Gamma has the most energy.'),
  ('All EM waves travel through a vacuum at…','the same speed (the speed of light).','different speeds.','the speed of sound.','About 300 000 km/s.')],
 [('Name the EM wave used in TV remote controls.','Infrared.'),
  ('Give two uses of X-rays and gamma rays.','X-rays: seeing broken bones / airport security.~Gamma: killing cancer cells / sterilising medical equipment.'),
  ('Explain why radiographers stand behind a screen when taking X-rays.','X-rays have high energy and can damage cells and DNA.~Radiographers take many X-rays every day, so their exposure would add up.~The lead screen absorbs X-rays and protects them.')])

ex('earth',"Igneous rock forms when magma or lava cools. Sedimentary rock forms from layers of sediment that are squashed and cemented. Metamorphic rock forms when heat and pressure change existing rock without melting it.",
 [('Which type of rock forms when magma cools?','Igneous.','Sedimentary.','Metamorphic.','Granite and basalt are igneous.'),
  ('Which layer of the Earth is liquid?','Outer core.','Inner core.','Crust.','The outer core is liquid metal.'),
  ('Marble forms from limestone by…','heat and pressure (metamorphism).','cooling lava.','weathering only.','Limestone is changed into marble.'),
  ('Which rock is most likely to contain fossils?','Sedimentary.','Igneous.','Metamorphic.','Sediment can bury and preserve organisms.')],
 [('What is the thin outer layer of the Earth called?','The crust.'),
  ('Granite has large crystals and basalt has small crystals. Explain the difference.','Granite cooled slowly underground, so crystals had time to grow.~Basalt cooled quickly from lava at the surface, so the crystals are small.'),
  ('Name the three types of rock and describe how each forms.','Igneous: from cooling magma or lava.~Sedimentary: layers of sediment compacted and cemented.~Metamorphic: existing rock changed by heat and pressure.')])

ex('sediment',"Freeze–thaw weathering happens when water in cracks freezes, expands and splits rock. Rivers carry sediment and drop it in layers when they slow down. Over time, layers are compacted and cemented into rocks such as sandstone and limestone.",
 [('What is freeze–thaw weathering?','Water in cracks freezes, expands and breaks the rock.','Rock melting in the sun.','Wind blowing rocks away.','Ice takes up more space than water.'),
  ('Erosion means…','rock pieces are carried away.','rock forms from lava.','rock changes by heat.','Water, wind and ice transport sediment.'),
  ('Which is a sedimentary rock?','Sandstone.','Granite.','Marble.','Made from sand grains cemented together.'),
  ('Why are fossils found in sedimentary rocks?','Organisms are buried by sediment and preserved.','Lava preserves animals.','Heat makes fossils.','Melting would destroy them.')],
 [('What is weathering?','The breakdown of rock into smaller pieces where it is.'),
  ('Describe two stages that turn loose sand into sandstone.','Layers of sand are buried and compacted by the weight above.~Minerals cement the grains together.'),
  ('Explain why a fossil found in a lower rock layer is usually older than one in a higher layer.','Sediment is deposited in layers over time.~New layers settle on top of older ones.~So lower layers, and the fossils in them, formed earlier.')])

ex('rock-cycle',"Rocks are recycled slowly over millions of years. Uplift can bring buried rocks back to the surface, where weathering starts again. The rock cycle is driven by the Sun (weathering, erosion) and by heat from inside the Earth.",
 [('How long do most rock cycle changes take?','Millions of years.','A few days.','One year.','Rock changes are very slow.'),
  ('What brings buried rocks back to the surface?','Uplift (movements of the Earth’s crust).','Rain.','Photosynthesis.','Plate movements push rocks up.'),
  ('Metamorphic rock that melts becomes…','magma.','sediment.','sandstone.','Melting forms magma.'),
  ('Which process breaks down rock at the surface?','Weathering.','Melting.','Compaction.','Weathering happens at the surface.')],
 [('What is magma?','Molten (melted) rock below the Earth’s surface.'),
  ('Describe how sedimentary rock can become metamorphic rock.','It is buried deep underground.~Heat and pressure change it without melting it.'),
  ('Explain how a piece of igneous rock could become part of a sedimentary rock.','The igneous rock is weathered into small pieces.~The pieces are eroded and transported, e.g. by rivers, and deposited in layers.~The layers are compacted and cemented into sedimentary rock.')])

ex('space',"Light from the Sun takes about 8 minutes to reach Earth. Distances in space are often measured in light years, the distance light travels in a year. The nearest star after the Sun is about 4 light years away.",
 [('What is a light year?','The distance light travels in one year.','The time light takes to travel 1 km.','A year on another planet.','It measures distance.'),
  ('The Sun is…','a star.','a planet.','a moon.','It produces its own light.'),
  ('Which is the largest?','The Universe.','A galaxy.','The Solar System.','Galaxies are in the Universe.'),
  ('About how long does sunlight take to reach Earth?','8 minutes.','8 seconds.','8 days.','Light is fast but the Sun is far.')],
 [('What is a galaxy?','A huge group of billions of stars (with gas and dust).'),
  ('Put in order of size, smallest first: galaxy, Earth, Universe, Solar System.','Earth, Solar System.~Galaxy, Universe.'),
  ('Explain why astronomers use light years instead of kilometres.','Distances between stars are enormous.~Numbers in kilometres would be huge and hard to use.~A light year is a much bigger unit, so the numbers are manageable.')])

ex('orbits',"In summer, the UK is tilted towards the Sun, so days are longer and the Sun is higher in the sky. Sunlight is more concentrated when the Sun is higher, so it feels warmer. In winter the UK is tilted away, so days are short and the Sun is low.",
 [('What causes the seasons?','The tilt of the Earth’s axis.','The Earth getting closer to the Sun.','The Moon blocking sunlight.','Distance changes very little.'),
  ('How long does Earth take to orbit the Sun?','About 365 days.','24 hours.','28 days.','One year.'),
  ('In UK summer, the northern hemisphere is…','tilted towards the Sun.','tilted away from the Sun.','not tilted.','Long days and high Sun.'),
  ('What causes day and night?','The Earth spinning on its axis.','The Earth orbiting the Sun.','The Moon orbiting Earth.','One spin takes about 24 hours.')],
 [('How long does the Earth take to spin once on its axis?','About 24 hours (one day).'),
  ('Give two differences between summer and winter in the UK.','Summer has longer days / shorter nights.~The Sun is higher in the sky in summer, so it feels warmer.'),
  ('Explain why it is summer in Australia when it is winter in the UK.','The Earth’s axis is tilted.~When the northern hemisphere tilts away from the Sun, the southern hemisphere tilts towards it.~So Australia gets longer days and higher Sun at the same time as the UK’s winter.')])

ex('moon',"A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth, blocking sunlight. A lunar eclipse happens when the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon. The Moon takes about 28 days to go through all its phases.",
 [('Why can we see the Moon?','It reflects sunlight.','It makes its own light.','It is on fire.','The Moon is not a light source.'),
  ('About how long is one cycle of Moon phases?','About 28 days.','About 24 hours.','About 365 days.','Roughly a month.'),
  ('A solar eclipse happens when…','the Moon blocks the Sun’s light from reaching part of the Earth.','Earth’s shadow falls on the Moon.','the Sun moves behind Earth.','The Moon is between Sun and Earth.'),
  ('A full Moon happens when…','the Moon is on the opposite side of Earth from the Sun.','the Moon is between Earth and the Sun.','Earth’s shadow covers it.','We see the whole lit half.')],
 [('What is a lunar eclipse?','When the Earth’s shadow falls on the Moon.'),
  ('Describe the positions of the Sun, Earth and Moon during a solar eclipse.','The Moon is between the Sun and the Earth.~They are in a straight line, so the Moon’s shadow falls on Earth.'),
  ('Explain why the Moon appears to change shape during a month.','The Sun always lights half of the Moon.~As the Moon orbits Earth, we see different amounts of the lit half.~This gives phases from new Moon to full Moon and back.')])

ex('friction',"Friction can be useful, such as grip on shoes and brakes on bikes. It can also be a nuisance, causing wear and wasted energy. Lubricants like oil reduce friction; streamlining reduces air resistance.",
 [('Which reduces friction between moving parts?','Oil (a lubricant).','Sand.','Rough surfaces.','Lubricants let surfaces slide.'),
  ('Air resistance on a cyclist can be reduced by…','crouching and wearing tight clothing (streamlining).','sitting upright with a coat open.','pedalling slower only.','A smaller frontal area reduces drag.'),
  ('Which is a useful effect of friction?','It lets car tyres grip the road.','It wears out engines.','It wastes fuel.','Without grip, cars would skid.'),
  ('Friction transfers energy to the…','thermal store of the surfaces.','chemical store.','nuclear store.','Rubbing hands warms them.')],
 [('What is drag?','A force that opposes motion through air or water.'),
  ('Give one useful and one unwanted effect of friction.','Useful: grip for walking/tyres/brakes.~Unwanted: wears parts / wastes energy as heat.'),
  ('Explain why racing cyclists wear smooth helmets and tight clothing.','They reduce the area and roughness facing the air.~This reduces air resistance (drag).~So more of the cyclist’s energy makes them go faster.')])

ex('spring',"Hooke's law says extension is proportional to force, up to the limit of proportionality. A force–extension graph is a straight line through the origin for a spring obeying Hooke's law. Beyond the elastic limit, a spring will not return to its original length.",
 [('A spring stretches 2 cm with 1 N. If it obeys Hooke’s law, how far with 3 N?','6 cm.','3 cm.','5 cm.','Extension is proportional to force.'),
  ('A force–extension graph for a spring obeying Hooke’s law is…','a straight line through the origin.','a curve from the start.','a horizontal line.','Proportional means a straight line through zero.'),
  ('What happens if a spring is stretched past its elastic limit?','It does not go back to its original length.','It gets shorter.','It becomes magnetic.','It is permanently deformed.'),
  ('Original length 10 cm, stretched length 14 cm. Extension?','4 cm.','24 cm.','14 cm.','14 − 10.')],
 [('What does Hooke’s law say?','The extension of a spring is proportional to the force applied (up to a limit).'),
  ('A spring extends 3 cm with a 2 N load. Calculate the extension with 6 N and state an assumption.','6 ÷ 2 = 3 times the force, so 3 × 3 = 9 cm.~Assume the spring has not passed its limit of proportionality.'),
  ('Describe how to investigate the extension of a spring.','Measure the original length with a ruler at eye level.~Add masses one at a time and measure the new length each time.~Calculate each extension and plot force against extension.')])

ex('pressure',"Small areas give large pressures, so knives are sharp and nails are pointed. Large areas give small pressures, so snowshoes and tractor tyres are wide. Pressure = force ÷ area.",
 [('A 600 N person stands on 0.02 m². What is the pressure?','30 000 Pa.','12 Pa.','620 Pa.','600 ÷ 0.02.'),
  ('Why do snowshoes stop you sinking into snow?','They spread your weight over a bigger area.','They make you lighter.','They are warm.','Bigger area, smaller pressure.'),
  ('Why is a knife sharp?','A small area gives a large pressure for cutting.','A sharp edge is lighter.','It reduces friction only.','Same force over a tiny area.'),
  ('What is the unit of pressure?','Pascal (Pa).','Newton (N).','Joule (J).','1 Pa = 1 N/m².')],
 [('Write the equation for pressure.','Pressure = force ÷ area.'),
  ('A box weighs 200 N and rests on an area of 0.5 m². Calculate the pressure.','P = 200 ÷ 0.5.~= 400 Pa.'),
  ('Explain why a stiletto heel can damage a floor but an elephant’s foot may not.','The stiletto heel has a tiny area.~Even a person’s small weight gives a very large pressure.~The elephant’s weight is spread over a large foot area, so the pressure can be smaller.')])

ex('liquid-pressure',"Dams are thicker at the bottom because water pressure is greater at depth. Divers feel more pressure the deeper they go. Hydraulic brakes and car jacks use liquid pressure to multiply force.",
 [('Why is a dam thicker at the bottom?','Water pressure is greater at greater depth.','To look stronger.','Because water is lighter at the bottom.','Pressure increases with depth.'),
  ('Liquid pressure acts…','in all directions.','only downwards.','only upwards.','Water pushes on all surfaces.'),
  ('A hydraulic jack can lift a car because…','pressure is passed through the liquid and acts on a larger piston.','liquids can be squashed easily.','oil is very light.','A larger area gives a larger force.'),
  ('Water spurts furthest from a hole…','near the bottom of a container.','near the top.','at the surface.','The pressure is greatest there.')],
 [('How does liquid pressure change with depth?','It increases with depth.'),
  ('Describe what you would see if three holes at different heights were made in a tall bottle of water, and why.','Water spurts out furthest from the lowest hole.~Because pressure is greater deeper down.'),
  ('Explain how hydraulic brakes in a car work.','The driver pushes a small piston, creating pressure in the brake fluid.~Liquids cannot be compressed, so the pressure is passed through the pipes.~It acts on larger pistons at the wheels, producing a bigger force to press the brake pads.')])
